<SubSection title="Change of Phase" id="ChangeofPhase">

<p> During a phase change, the additional or removal of thermal energy results in a transition between states (solid, liquid, gas). Phase changes occur at specific temperatures. </p>

 <Image src="/transfer/phase_diagram.png" width="4">
        <span class="fw-bold">Phase Diagram</span>: The three phases and phase transitions are dependent on temperature and pressure. At the middle is the triple point, where the three phases coexist in thermodynamic equilibrium.
    </Image>

<p> Phase changes complicate heat transfer, as the process is energy intensive and causes changes in the properties of the material. 
For example, consider the following properties affected with the cooling of water from vapor to liquid to ice:
    
    <Itemize>
        <Item> <i> Density increases: </i> Solids have the highest density, since atoms are tightly packed together. </Item>
        <Item> <i> Volume decreases: </i>  As the density of a material increases, volume decreases. </Item>
        <Item> <i> Thermal conductivity increases: </i> The thermal conductivity of ice is nearly three times greater than water. </Item>
        <Item> <i> Specific heat slightly decreases: </i> Generally, <InlineEquation equation="C_{p, gas} < C_{p, solid} \\leq C_{p, liquid}" />. Intuitively, liquids buffer temperature best. </Item>
        <Item> <i> Dielectric properties (i.e. the interaction of a material with electromagnetic waves) decrease: </i> For example, frozen food takes much longer to microwave, due to poor microwave absorption. </Item>
        <Item> <i> Elastic modulus decreases: </i> Typically, materials become more brittle at colder temperatures. </Item>
    </Itemize>
    
    <p> As a material changes phase, so does the energy of the system. The <b> latent heat of fusion (<InlineEquation equation="\\Delta H_{fus}"/>) </b> 
	describes the enthalpy difference due to a change of phase from solid to liquid, which occurs at melting temperature. 
	Conversely, the <b> latent heat of vaporization (<InlineEquation equation="\\Delta H_{vap}"/>) </b> describes the enthalpy difference due to a change of phase from liquid to vapor, 
	which occurs at the boiling temperature. </p>
    
   
    <Image src="/transfer/enthalpy_diagram_water.png" width="5">
        <span class="fw-bold">Enthalpy Diagram</span>: Enthalpy changes in water, showing the latent heats of fusion and vaporization at melting and freezing point temperatures. 
    </Image>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Freezing and Thawing" id="FreezeThaw">
</SubSection>


<SubSubSection title="Pure Water" id="PureWater">

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
    <CalloutCard title="Need a review?">
     <p> Be sure to use the appropriate <a href="/transfer/Conduction/#CharacteristicLength">characteristic length</a> for the geometry! </p>
    </CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>


	<p> Pure water freezes through four sequential stages: </p>
	<ol>
		<li> <i>Supercooling:</i> water cools to less than 0°C before any ice forms. Ultra-pure water may not freeze until −40°C! </li>
		<li> <i>Nucleation:</i> nuclei (crystalline seeds that act as a foundation for ice crystal growth) spontaneously generate. </li>
		<li> <i>Crystal growth:</i> ice crystals grow, controlled by the rate at which heat is removed from the system </li>
		<li> <i>Maturation:</i> smaller ice crystals decrease in size, and larger crystals grow. The number of crystals may decrease, but average crystal size increases! </li>
	</ol>
	
	<p> If the object is frozen from the outside in, the freezing time depends on geometry:
	
	<b><DisplayEquation equation="t_{f} = \\frac{\\Delta H_{f} \\rho}{T_{m}-T_{\\infty} } (\\frac{L^{2}}{2k} \\frac {L}{h})" title="Slab Geometry" background="True"/></b>
	<b><DisplayEquation equation="t_{f} = \\frac{\\Delta H_{f} \\rho}{T_{m}-T_{\\infty} } (\\frac{R^{2}}{4k} \\frac {R}{2h})" title="Cylindrical Geometry" background="True"/></b>
	<b><DisplayEquation equation="t_{f} = \\frac{\\Delta H_{f} \\rho}{T_{m}-T_{\\infty} } (\\frac{R^{2}}{6k} \\frac {R}{3h})" title="Spherical Geometry" background="True"/></b>

	where 
	<Itemize>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="t_f" /> = freezing time <InlineEquation equation="[s]" /></Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="\\Delta H_f" /> = latent heat of fusion <InlineEquation equation="[\\frac{J}{kg}]" />. 
		(<i>Note: for biological tissue with a water component, the latent heat of fusion of water is multiplied by the the water content fraction in the tissue.</i>) </Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="\\rho" /> = density <InlineEquation equation="[\\frac{kg}{m^3}]" /></Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_m" /> = melting or freezing temperature <InlineEquation equation="[K]" /></Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_{\\infty}" /> = surrounding fluid temperature <InlineEquation equation="[K]" /></Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="L" /> or <InlineEquation equation="R" /> = characteristic length, depending on geometry <InlineEquation equation="[m]" /></Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="k" /> = thermal conductivity of the medium <InlineEquation equation="[\\frac{W}{mK}]" /></Item>
	<Item><InlineEquation equation="h" /> = convective heat transfer coefficient <InlineEquation equation="[\\frac{W}{m^2 K}]" /></Item>
	</Itemize>

	<p> Looking at key parameter relationships, freezing time <i>decreases</i> with
    <Itemize>
        <Item> higher thermal conductivity <InlineEquation equation="k" /> (less resistance to heat flow) </Item>
        <Item> higher convective coefficient <InlineEquation equation="h" /> (stronger surface cooling) </Item>
        <Item> larger temperature difference <InlineEquation equation="\\Delta T" /> (stronger driving force) </Item>
        <Item> smaller characteristic length <InlineEquation equation="L" /> (there is more mass to freeze) </Item>
    </Itemize>

	<p> The frozen layer thickness grows as <InlineEquation equation="x \\propto \\sqrt{t}" />; freezing progressively slows as the frozen layer thickens because it adds thermal resistance. </p>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Heterogeneous Solutions" id="Solutions">
	<p> Solutions behave differently than pure water. Because solutes are present, heterogeneous nuclei form more readily, so nucleation occurs earlier and less supercooling is required. </p>
	<p> When solutes (e.g., salt) dissolve in a solvent (e.g, water), they interfere with hydrogen bonding, depressing the freezing point below 0°C. 
	The relationship is expressed as: </p>
	<DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{\\Delta H_{f}}{R_{g}} [\\frac{1}{T_{pure}} - \\frac{1}{T_{soln}}] = ln(x_{A})" title="" background="True"/>
	
where 
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="\\Delta H_{f}" /> = latent heat of fusion of pure liquid <InlineEquation equation="[\\frac{J}{kg}]" /></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="R_{g}" /> = ideal gas constant, <InlineEquation equation="8.314 [\\frac{J}{mol K}]" /></Item> 
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_{pure}" /> = freezing point of pure liquid <InlineEquation equation="[K]" /></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T_{soln}" /> = freezing point of the solution <InlineEquation equation="[K]" /></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="x_{A}" /> = mole fraction of solvent A (e.g., water) in solution </Item>
</Itemize>

<p> As freezing proceeds, water crystallizes out of solution, leaving behind a progressively more concentrated liquid, which in turn depresses the freezing point further. 
	This process continues until all freezable water has solidified. 
	Practically, this means biological solutions freeze gradually over a temperature range (roughly −2 to −35°C) rather than at a single sharp temperature. </p>

</SubSubSection>


<SubSubSection title="Biological Materials" id="Biological Materials">
		<p> Cell size and integrity are affected by the freezing process.</p>
		<p> Ice nucleation begins in the extracellular space first, concentrating the fluid; conversely, the plasma membrane inhibits the growth of ice crystals in the cytoplasm. This generates an osmotic pressure than causes water to flow out of the cell to freeze in the extracellular space, dehydrating the cell. </p> 
		
		<Image src="/transfer/cell_freezing.png" width="4">
			<span class="fw-bold">Cell freezing by speed</span>: the rate of cell dehydration and occurrence of intracellular crystallization. (image source)
		</Image>
	
	<p> These effects are depend on the speed at which the cells are frozen: </p>
	
	<Itemize>
		<Item> <i> Slower rate: </i> results in higher osmotic water migration, leading to cell dehydration and shrinkage; cell membranes remain intact. </Item>
		<Item> <i> Faster rate: </i> results in less water migration, so the cell remains closer to original shape and size. Leads to the formation of intracellular ice, which damages cell membranes. </Item>
		<Item> <i> Rapid rate: </i> Little to no water migration, causing the cell to retain its original shape. Causes the most formation of intracellular ice and thermal expansion effects, damaging cell membranes. </Item>
	</Itemize>

</SubSubSection>

